WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.432
Rate limits and 429s.

2
00:00:02.712 --> 00:00:16.515
Anthropic measures rate limits per model class in requests per minute, input tokens per minute, and output tokens per minute, enforced with a token bucket: capacity refills continuously rather than resetting on the minute.

3
00:00:16.795 --> 00:00:19.291
The docs add two details that change design.

4
00:00:19.571 --> 00:00:27.613
A limit of 60 requests per minute may be enforced as one per second, so bursts can trip it even when the per-minute average is fine.

5
00:00:27.893 --> 00:00:36.597
And sharp increases in usage can hit acceleration limits, so ramp traffic up gradually, for example when you launch a feature or start a backfill.

6
00:00:36.877 --> 00:00:38.691
Three practical consequences.

7
00:00:38.971 --> 00:00:51.323
First, throttle yourself before the provider does: a concurrency limit or a local token bucket in front of the model call turns a burst into a short queue instead of a wall of 429s.

8
00:00:51.603 --> 00:01:03.677
Second, read the headers: responses include anthropic-ratelimit-* headers with remaining requests and tokens and reset times, which you can log and alert on before you hit the wall.

9
00:01:03.957 --> 00:01:19.381
Third, caching raises your effective limit: the docs say the default input-token limits count only uncached input tokens, so cache reads don’t count, so a well-cached system prompt is a throughput feature as well as a cost one (week 2).

10
00:01:19.661 --> 00:01:21.603
Separate models have separate buckets.

11
00:01:21.883 --> 00:01:25.616
On the review date, the rate-limits page lists Claude Opus 5.

12
00:01:25.896 --> 00:01:27.731
5 and Claude Sonnet 5.

13
00:01:28.011 --> 00:01:32.405
5 with their own limits, not shared with older models in the same family.

14
00:01:32.685 --> 00:01:38.275
That is one reason a fallback to another model often succeeds when the primary is rate limited.
